  "baseRefName": "main",
  "body": "Fixes #190.\n\n## The regression\n\n`clients setup` mints a router token (`issue_client_token`) and writes it into a 0600 environment file, but nothing recorded *which* token it was. `ClientManager::remove` only deleted files, so the minted credential stayed `revoked=false` in the token store forever — anyone holding a copy of the environment file kept working access to the router.\n\nReproduce on `main`:\n\n```bash\nlink-assistant-router clients setup codex\nlink-assistant-router clients remove codex\nlink-assistant-router tokens list   # the client-codex token is still revoked=false\n```\n\n`with` and `with --global` were checked and were already correct: per-run credentials are revoked by `cleanup_run_credential`, and `--global` persists no credential of its own.\n\n## The fix\n\n- **Metadata.** `TokenManager::issue_with_id` returns the record id alongside the JWT, and setup persists a secret-free `ManagedCredential` (`<client>.credential.json`, mode 0600) recording `source` (`minted`/`supplied`), `token_id`, `label`, `issued_at`. The token itself is never written there — only the id, which is enough to revoke and useless to an attacker. It is written *after* the environment file, so a recorded id always describes a secret that exists on disk.\n- **Atomic removal.** `clients remove` revokes first and deletes second. Deleting first would leave a live credential nobody can name any more, which is the regression itself.\n- **User-supplied tokens are not revoked** unless `--revoke-supplied` is passed; the same token is often shared with other machines. If `--revoke-supplied` is given for a token this router cannot recognise, the command errors rather than silently doing nothing.\n- **Fails closed.** When revocation fails, the credential file stays in place, the command prints the token id plus `tokens revoke <ID>` recovery instructions, and exits 1 — it never reports successful removal. `--force` is the documented escape hatch.\n\n## Tests\n\nNew `tests/clients_token_revocation_test.rs` drives the shipped binary end to end, for every client integration that supports permanent setup (codex, claude-code, opencode, qwen-code, agent, grok-cli) and both persistent storage backends (`text`, `binary`):\n\n- `setup_show_remove_revokes_the_minted_token_for_every_client_and_backend` — setup → `show` reports the credential → `remove` prints `revoked managed token <ID>` → a **fresh process** runs `tokens list` and sees `revoked=true`. The new process reads the store from disk, so this is the restart-persistence check too.\n- `a_removed_client_credential_is_rejected_by_a_live_router` — a real `serve` process accepts `POST /v1/chat/completions` with the credential before removal and answers 401/403 after it, on both backends.\n- `supplied_tokens_survive_remove_unless_revocation_is_requested`.\n- `failed_revocation_keeps_the_credential_and_exits_nonzero` — store made unreadable; asserts nonzero exit, recovery instructions, and that both the environment file and the metadata survive; then `--force` succeeds.\n- `credential_metadata_never_contains_the_token` — no `la_sk_` on disk, mode 0600.\n\nPlus unit tests in `src/clients/credentials.rs` for the revocability rule and the disk round trip.\n\nFull local checks pass: `cargo fmt`, `cargo clippy --all-targets --all-features` (clean), file-size limits, and the entire `cargo test` suite.\n",
